WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.130
Imagine new data showing that people whose first language is

2
00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:06.870
not English are approved for a support service at much

3
00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:09.790
lower rates than everyone else. When the numbers are shared,

4
00:00:10.130 --> 00:00:13.070
some managers feel the data is accusing them personally. The

5
00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:13.590
room is tense.

6
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:16.480
That tension is real, and I think it is one

7
00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.520
reason hard data sometimes stays in a drawer. So what

8
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.720
kind of leadership does the page describe for a moment

9
00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:22.580
like this one?

10
00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:29.040
Transformative leadership, also called equity centered leadership. It examines power

11
00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.960
and redesigns the systems that produce unequal results, both inside

12
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:36.660
the organization and in the communities it serves. Its starting

13
00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.620
point is that rules and budgets which look neutral on

14
00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:43.100
paper can still produce unequal outcomes, and that those outcomes

15
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.020
are design flaws leadership is responsible for fixing.

16
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.160
The names are confusing, I have to admit. Transformative and

17
00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.620
transformational sound almost identical. How are they different from each

18
00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:53.840
other?

19
00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:58.870
They are easy to mix up. Transformational leadership focuses on

20
00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:04.750
improving performance, innovation and culture. Transformative leadership focuses explicitly on

21
00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:08.350
fairness and power. It asks who is being served well,

22
00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:12.230
who is not, and why. The scholar Carolyn Shields is

23
00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:13.790
closely associated with it.

24
00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.700
What would I notice about a transformative leader in practice?

25
00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:20.980
I imagine it is more than speaking up about equity

26
00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:21.500
in meetings.

27
00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:26.790
They look beneath averages. If satisfaction is high overall, they

28
00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.730
ask who sits in the group that is not being

29
00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:32.250
served well, and what barriers they meet. They design programs

30
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.890
with the people affected, not only for them. They name

31
00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:39.590
barriers plainly, while focusing on redesign rather than blame. Equity

32
00:01:39.590 --> 00:01:41.990
priorities show up in real budgets and staffing.

33
00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:46.310
And success is measured by smaller gaps, not by the

34
00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:47.330
number of trainings held.

35
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:52.420
Where is this style most valuable? Public service seems like

36
00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.960
the obvious answer, but I would love to hear why.

37
00:01:55.570 --> 00:01:59.790
Because in human services, structural patterns shape who gets help

38
00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:03.130
and who falls through the cracks. Redesigning the underlying rules,

39
00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:07.230
such as eligibility steps, digital accessibility, language access and hiring

40
00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:11.730
criteria, produces improvements that outlast any single initiative. The page

41
00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.630
also mentions the curb cut effect: designing for people facing

42
00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:17.590
the most barriers often makes services easier for everyone.

43
00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:20.340
And yet this is hard work, and it does not

44
00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.600
always go well. Where does the page say transformative leadership

45
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:24.500
tends to struggle?

46
00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:29.990
Challenging long standing norms creates friction. A leader who relies

47
00:02:29.990 --> 00:02:33.830
on urgency alone, without building allies or giving managers practical

48
00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:39.030
tools, can become isolated. Redesign takes years, and without visible

49
00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.050
short term wins people lose patience.

50
00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:44.270
There is also a risk of focusing so much on

51
00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:46.910
the right words that people freeze, afraid of saying the

52
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:49.090
wrong thing, and practical progress slows.

53
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:53.480
The page describes this style as the direct engine of

54
00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.120
systemic equity. How does it compare with inclusive leadership, which

55
00:02:57.120 --> 00:02:58.700
we often hear about in the same breath?

56
00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:03.010
Inclusive leadership shapes whether people feel they belong from day

57
00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:06.550
to day. Transformative leadership changes the structures that decide who

58
00:03:06.550 --> 00:03:10.110
has access and influence. Together they are powerful. On its

59
00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:14.550
own, transformative work moves beyond awareness workshops to concrete changes,

60
00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:19.530
such as removing unnecessary degree requirements, redesigning promotion criteria and

61
00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.250
building accessibility into every public portal from the start.

62
00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:26.400
You mentioned managers feeling blamed. That seems to be the

63
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.060
biggest practical risk for this style. How does the page

64
00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:29.600
address it?

65
00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:35.130
Directly. If senior leaders set bold equity expectations without giving

66
00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:39.210
supervisors clear tools, realistic workloads and safe places to learn,

67
00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:43.430
supervisors feel blamed or overwhelmed, and they become quiet bottlenecks

68
00:03:43.430 --> 00:03:46.350
instead of partners. Equipping people is part of the work,

69
00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:47.310
not an extra.

70
00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:50.760
So back to the tense room and the approval data.

71
00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:53.800
What would a strong transformative response look like in that

72
00:03:53.800 --> 00:03:54.060
meeting?

73
00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:58.350
Share the data as a system problem rather than individual

74
00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:01.510
blame, and work with the managers to trace where in

75
00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:05.130
the process the gap appears and what to change. That

76
00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:08.270
equips them instead of putting them on trial. Holding the

77
00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:10.990
data back until it has been studied further would protect

78
00:04:10.990 --> 00:04:13.350
comfort while the unequal approvals continue.

79
00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:17.120
What else does the page suggest for leaders who want

80
00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.340
to work this way, especially without burning out themselves or

81
00:04:20.340 --> 00:04:21.080
the people around them?

82
00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:26.390
Translate equity into concrete tools: plain language guides, structured interview

83
00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:31.610
questions and accessibility checklists. Celebrate practical wins, like faster accommodations

84
00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:35.810
or simpler intake forms, to build momentum. And anchor equity

85
00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:40.310
goals in strategic plans, budgets and leadership evaluations, so the

86
00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:42.870
work belongs to the institution and not to one champion.

87
00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.080
Here is a question to carry with you. When you

88
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:49.040
name an unfair result, do people walk away equipped, or

89
00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:50.720
blamed? Thank you for listening.
